.TH "pocket_web" "1" "" "" "Pocket SDR"
.SH NAME
pocket_web \- GNSS receiver server with Web UI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
pocket_web [\-web [addr:]port] [\-html dir] [\-ini file] [\-start]
           [\-debug file] [\-v]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a GNSS receiver as a server controlled from a Web UI in a browser.
Static Web UI files are served over HTTP, and commands and monitor data are exchanged over WebSocket (see \f[CR]doc/design_web_ui.md\f[R]).
The receiver itself provides the same signal tracking, navigation data decoding, and PVT generation as \f[CR]pocket_trk\f[R].
.PP
The AP does not start the receiver by itself unless \f[CR]\-start\f[R] is given.
The input source, output streams, signal selection, and system options are all set from the Web UI, and the receiver is started and stopped from there.
The settings are restored from \f[CR]\-ini file\f[R] at startup and saved when the receiver is stopped and when the AP exits, so a session continues where the previous one left off.
The AP prints no runtime status of its own; the receiver is monitored from the Web UI.
.PP
The acquisition mode \f[CR]Fast\f[R] of the Web UI (receiver option \f[CR]\-FAST_SRCH\f[R], also selectable in \f[CR]pocket_sdr.py\f[R] and by the \f[CR]fast_acq\f[R] system option key of
\f[CR]pocket_trk\f[R]) predicts satellite visibility and Doppler at the receiver time from the navigation data, almanac, and last fix position saved in \f[CR].pocket_navdata.csv\f[R].
Until the TOW and the week number of a received signal set the receiver time, the signals are acquired by the full search as in the \f[CR]Full\f[R] mode.
.SH OPTIONS ([]: DEFAULT)
.TP
\f[CR]\-web [addr:]port\f[R]
TCP port of the Web UI server, and optionally the bind address.
The address defaults to \f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R] (loopback only); specify \f[CR]0.0.0.0:port\f[R] to accept LAN clients.
The interface is unauthenticated \- do not expose it to untrusted networks.
Required.
.TP
\f[CR]\-html dir\f[R]
Document root of the Web UI files.
[\f[CR]<exe_dir>/../html\f[R]]
.TP
\f[CR]\-ini file\f[R]
Settings file for the receiver configuration and the system options.
[\f[CR]pocket_web.ini\f[R]]
.TP
\f[CR]\-start\f[R]
Start the receiver at startup with the restored settings, as the Start command of the Web UI does.
On error the AP keeps running so that the settings can be fixed and the receiver started from the Web UI.
[start stopped]
.TP
\f[CR]\-debug file\f[R]
Enable RTKLIB trace output to the given file (trace level 3).
.TP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R]
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
pocket_web \-web 0.0.0.0:8080
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]run_sdr.sh\f[R] in the top directory starts and stops the server.
On MSYS2 it stops it with SIGQUIT, which reaches a native Windows process as CTRL_BREAK_EVENT; a plain \f[CR]kill\f[R] is \f[CR]TerminateProcess()\f[R] and would leave the RF frontend streaming, which
needs a USB reset to recover.
.SH HTTPS WITH A REVERSE PROXY (REFERENCE)
The Web UI has neither TLS nor authentication of its own.
For access outside a trusted LAN, keep \f[CR]pocket_web\f[R] bound to loopback and put a reverse proxy in front of it.
The proxy only has to forward the WebSocket upgrade; everything else is plain HTTP proxying.
.IP
.EX
pocket_web \-web 8080          # loopback only; the proxy is the only client
.EE
.PP
Caddy [1] issues and renews the certificate itself and forwards WebSockets without extra configuration:
.IP
.EX
sdr.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
.EE
.PP
nginx [2] needs the upgrade headers passed through explicitly:
.IP
.EX
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name sdr.example.com;
    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/sdr.example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/sdr.example.com/privkey.pem;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade    $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection \(dqupgrade\(dq;
        proxy_set_header Host       $host;
        proxy_read_timeout 3600s;   # the monitor WebSocket is long\-lived
    }
}
.EE
.PP
Notes:
.IP \(bu 2
Add authentication at the proxy (\f[CR]auth_basic\f[R] for nginx, \f[CR]basic_auth\f[R] for Caddy).
TLS alone only hides the traffic; anyone reaching the proxy can still start, stop and reconfigure the receiver.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]proxy_read_timeout\f[R] has to outlast an idle WebSocket.
The default 60 s is enough while a page pushes data, but a client sitting on a page with no subscription would be disconnected.
.IP \(bu 2
The browser upgrades to \f[CR]wss:\f[R] by itself, since the Web UI derives the WebSocket scheme from the page URL.
No option change is needed.
.IP \(bu 2
A public certificate needs a public domain name.
On a closed LAN, use a local CA instead, such as Caddy\(cqs internal CA (\f[CR]tls internal\f[R]) or mkcert [3]; certbot [4] is the usual client for public certificates.
.PP
